From e24af05fcdc087ecc73c860de302d3b5c42365e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:09:09 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] DIV/IDIV: #DE on quotient overflow, divisor read once
 (MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE)

x86 raises #DE not only for a zero divisor but also when the quotient does not fit the destination;
AArch64 UDIV/SDIV and the 128-bit helpers silently truncate it. With MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE=1
(default off) DIVOp/IDIVOp build the full condition without dividing, zero divisor included:
DIV faults iff the high half of the dividend >= the divisor (unsigned); IDIV faults iff
|dividend| >= |divisor| * 2^(n-1), plus |divisor| for a negative quotient; for r64 a 128-bit compare.
The divisor is read once: check, division and write-back stay in one block, RAX/RDX keep their old
values through a select when #DE is due, and the #DE branch follows the write-back. The division then
gets divisor 1, so LDIV/LUDIV never see /0 or INT128_MIN / -1. Without the gate the existing zero check
(which reads a memory divisor twice) is unchanged.

MacRunnerDivOverflowChecks counts translations with the full check; a macrunner-fex-div-overflow-de line
is printed at 1, 2, 4, ... HB<->FEX oracle (72 219 cases, product and native feature sets, one binary for
both arms): 26 of 26 overflow cases become x86-correct, no other result changes, 73 non-overflowing
DIV/IDIV cases stay correct. Windows reports a quotient overflow as STATUS_INTEGER_OVERFLOW (0xC0000095,
measured under Prism); the frontends still report every #DE as EXCEPTION_INT_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO.

